# Line endings are decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# Without this file, each contributor's `core.autocrlf` decides what bytes land
# in a commit. The same file then arrives differently on different machines and
# shows up as a whole-file diff nobody wrote — which is especially easy here,
# where the host is Windows (autocrlf on) and the client under test is Ubuntu.

# Anything Git recognises as text is stored as LF *and checked out as LF*, on
# every platform. `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: on its own, `text=auto`
# still hands Windows a CRLF working copy when autocrlf is on, so the checkout
# and the repository disagree about a file neither side edited.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exceptions, for files something other than an editor has to parse.
# cmd.exe genuinely mis-parses a .bat/.cmd whose lines end in a bare LF, and a
# .sh checked out with CRLF dies on Linux with "bad interpreter: no such file".
# .ps1 tolerates either; it is here so Windows scripts stay consistent.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sh  text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never rewrites bytes inside them and never prints a diff that
# is megabytes of noise. The compiled CLI binaries in apps/cli/dist are ignored
# rather than tracked, but this is an app that serves images, so one arriving in
# the repository by hand is a matter of when.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.exe    binary
*.sqlite binary
